On October 21, 2026, the PHP project shipped an emergency security release for two critical, unauthenticated remote code execution flaws in PHP core, tracked as **CVE-2026-109618** and **CVE-2026-109619** and nicknamed **PHP2Shell** . Both carry a CVSS v4.0 score of 9.2. The bugs were discovered by Calif, Wiz Research and OpenAI through OpenAI's "Patch the Planet" initiative, coordinated with the PHP security team.


The "patch now" message has been covered everywhere, and you should patch now. This piece is for the engineer who has already done that and wants to understand what actually broke, why it sat in the codebase since PHP 5.4.0, why your deployment's exposure depends on whether you run mod_php or PHP-FPM, and how to reason about this class of bug in your own code review.


> **A note on scope and attribution.** CodeAnt did not discover these vulnerabilities and has not independently reproduced them. The analysis below synthesizes the public technical disclosures from the PHP project, Calif (Ryan Austin), Wiz Research, Sansec, JFrog, AWS and others, with links in the Sources section. Where the public writeups disagree on which CVE maps to which code primitive, we say so rather than invent precision. Code excerpts are drawn from PHP's open-source tree and the published research, reproduced here only to explain the mechanism.


